const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 10 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 10 — A Memory of Solferino: Henry Dunant and International Humanitarian Law"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Origin and sources — Dunant, the Lieber Code, Geneva and Hague law, the 1949 Conventions and the 1977 Protocols, customary IHL · Basic principles — distinction, military necessity, proportionality, humanity, precaution, the Martens clause · The four Geneva Conventions · Rights of prisoners of war (GC III) · Rights of civilians (GC IV and AP I) · Common Art 3 and non-international armed conflict (AP II) · Women and IHL · IHL and human rights law · India and IHL — the Geneva Conventions Act 1960"));
c.push(STORY("Solferino, Lombardy, 24 June 1859. A Swiss businessman, Henry Dunant, had come to northern Italy to petition Napoleon III about a business concession in Algeria, and found himself at the edge of the largest battle Europe had seen since Waterloo — 300,000 men of the French-Sardinian and Austrian armies, and by nightfall 40,000 dead and wounded on the field. The armies' medical services collapsed; the wounded lay for days; the Austrians who could not move were bayoneted. Dunant organised the women of the nearby town of Castiglione to nurse the wounded of both sides — 'tutti fratelli', all brothers, they said — bought supplies with his own money and persuaded the French to release captured Austrian doctors. In 1862 he published, at his own expense, 'A Memory of Solferino', which ended with two proposals: that every country form a society of trained volunteers to care for the wounded in war, and that the nations agree a treaty to protect the wounded and those who tend them. The Geneva Society for Public Welfare formed the Committee of Five — Dunant, the jurist Gustave Moynier, General Dufour and two doctors — in February 1863; the International Conference of October 1863 created the national societies and the emblem; and on 22 August 1864 twelve States signed the Geneva Convention for the Amelioration of the Condition of the Wounded in Armies in the Field — ten articles that made ambulances, hospitals and medical personnel neutral and required the wounded of every side to be collected and cared for. Dunant went bankrupt, was expelled from the Committee, lived in obscurity for thirty years and shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901. His two proposals became the Red Cross and the Geneva Conventions, and his book's rule — that the enemy who can no longer fight is a man to be cared for — is the first principle of the law this episode describes."));
c.push(H3("Origin and sources"));
c.push(P("**International humanitarian law (IHL)** — the law of armed conflict, the jus in bello (as distinct from the jus ad bellum, the law on the resort to force under the UN Charter's Arts 2(4) and 51) — is the body of rules which, for humanitarian reasons, limits the effects of armed conflict by protecting persons who are not or are no longer participating in hostilities and by restricting the means and methods of warfare. Its two historical streams: **Geneva law** (the protection of victims — the wounded, sick, shipwrecked, prisoners, civilians) and **Hague law** (the conduct of hostilities — weapons, targets, methods), merged in the 1977 Protocols. **Sources**: (1) the **Lieber Code (1863)** — Francis Lieber's instructions for the Union armies, the first modern codification; (2) the **Geneva Convention of 1864** (revised 1906, 1929, 1949); the **St Petersburg Declaration (1868)** — no explosive bullets under 400 grams, and the principle that the only legitimate object of war is to weaken the enemy's military forces and that weapons causing unnecessary suffering are prohibited; (3) the **Hague Conventions of 1899 and 1907** — the Regulations on the Laws and Customs of War on Land (Hague IV, 1907 — belligerents, POWs, hostilities, occupation) and the **Martens clause** in the preamble; the prohibitions of dum-dum bullets and asphyxiating gases; (4) the **Geneva Protocol of 1925** on gas and bacteriological weapons; the **1929 Geneva Conventions** on the wounded and on POWs; (5) the **four Geneva Conventions of 12 August 1949** (in force 21 October 1950; 196 parties — universal): I — wounded and sick in the field; II — wounded, sick and shipwrecked at sea; III — prisoners of war; IV — civilians; (6) the **Additional Protocols of 8 June 1977**: I — international armed conflicts (174 parties; India, the United States, Pakistan, Israel, Iran, Turkey are not parties); II — non-international armed conflicts (169 parties; India not a party); and **AP III (2005)** on the red crystal; (7) the weapons treaties — the **Biological Weapons Convention (1972)**, the **Conventional Weapons Convention (1980)** with its Protocols (non-detectable fragments, mines and booby-traps, incendiaries, blinding lasers, explosive remnants), the **Chemical Weapons Convention (1993)**, the **Ottawa Convention on anti-personnel mines (1997)**, the **Cluster Munitions Convention (2008)**, the **Arms Trade Treaty (2013)**, the **Nuclear Weapons Prohibition Treaty (2017)**; (8) the **Hague Convention on Cultural Property (1954)** with its 1999 Protocol; (9) the **Rome Statute (1998)** — war crimes in Art 8; (10) **customary IHL** — the ICRC's 2005 study of 161 rules, most applicable in both international and non-international conflicts, binding on all States (the ICJ's Nuclear Weapons opinion, 1996: the Hague and Geneva rules are 'intransgressible principles of international customary law'); (11) the **Martens clause** (Hague 1899, GC common Art 63/62/142/158, AP I Art 1(2)): in cases not covered by treaty, civilians and combatants remain under the protection and authority of the principles of international law derived from established custom, from the principles of humanity and from the dictates of public conscience; (12) military manuals, judicial decisions (Nuremberg, the ICTY — Tadić, 1995, on the definition of armed conflict; the ICTR; the ICC) and the ICRC's Commentaries."));
c.push(H3("Basic principles"));
c.push(P("(1) **Distinction** — the parties must at all times distinguish between the civilian population and combatants, and between civilian objects and military objectives, and direct operations only against military objectives (AP I Art 48 — the 'basic rule'; Art 51(2) — the civilian population as such and individual civilians shall not be the object of attack; Art 52(2) — military objectives are objects which by their nature, location, purpose or use make an effective contribution to military action and whose destruction offers a definite military advantage; Art 51(4) — indiscriminate attacks prohibited; civilians lose protection 'for such time as they take a direct part in hostilities' — Art 51(3)); the corollary of **combatant status** — members of the armed forces (other than medical and religious personnel) are combatants who may participate directly in hostilities and, if captured, are POWs (AP I Arts 43-44); (2) **military necessity** — the use of only that degree and kind of force required to achieve the legitimate purpose of the conflict, the complete or partial submission of the enemy at the earliest moment with the minimum expenditure of life and resources (the Lieber Code Art 14; Hague Art 23(g) — no destruction or seizure of enemy property unless imperatively demanded by the necessities of war); necessity operates within the law, not above it (the Nuremberg Hostages case rejected Kriegsraison); (3) **proportionality** — an attack is prohibited if it may be expected to cause incidental loss of civilian life, injury to civilians, damage to civilian objects, or a combination, which would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated (AP I Arts 51(5)(b) and 57(2)(a)(iii)); (4) **humanity / the prohibition of unnecessary suffering** — it is prohibited to employ weapons, projectiles and material and methods of warfare of a nature to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering (AP I Art 35(2); Hague Art 23(e); St Petersburg); the prohibition of means that cause widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment (Art 35(3), 55); no quarter, perfidy, and the killing of the hors de combat prohibited (Arts 37, 40, 41); (5) **precaution** — constant care to spare civilians; precautions in attack (verify targets, choose means to minimise civilian harm, cancel if disproportionate, effective advance warning where circumstances permit — Art 57) and against the effects of attacks (remove civilians from military objectives, no human shields — Art 58, 51(7)); (6) **the protection of the hors de combat and of persons in the power of the enemy** — humane treatment without adverse distinction (common Art 3; GC III Art 13; GC IV Art 27); (7) **the limitation of methods and means** — 'the right of belligerents to adopt means of injuring the enemy is not unlimited' (Hague Art 22; AP I Art 35(1)); (8) **neutrality and impartiality** of medical services and of the ICRC; (9) **the equal application** of IHL to all parties regardless of the justice of their cause (the separation of jus in bello from jus ad bellum); (10) **the Martens clause**. The ICJ's **Nuclear Weapons Advisory Opinion (1996)** named distinction and unnecessary suffering as the 'cardinal principles', held IHL applicable to nuclear weapons, and could not conclude definitively whether their use in an extreme circumstance of self-defence would be lawful."));
c.push(H3("The four Geneva Conventions of 1949 — the scheme"));
c.push(P("**Common articles**: **Art 1** — the parties undertake to respect and to ensure respect for the Conventions in all circumstances (the erga omnes duty — the Wall opinion, 2004); **Art 2** — the Conventions apply to declared war or any other armed conflict between two or more parties even if the state of war is not recognised, and to all cases of partial or total occupation even if it meets no armed resistance — the **international armed conflict (IAC)**; **Art 3** — the 'Convention in miniature' for **non-international armed conflict** (below); the articles on Protecting Powers and the ICRC's substitute role (8-11 / 9-11); the **grave breaches** regime (GC I Arts 49-50, II 50-51, III 129-130, IV 146-147) — the parties must enact penal legislation, search for and try or extradite persons who commit grave breaches (wilful killing, torture or inhuman treatment, biological experiments, wilfully causing great suffering or serious injury, extensive destruction and appropriation of property not justified by military necessity, compelling a POW or protected person to serve in hostile forces, depriving them of a fair trial, unlawful deportation, transfer or confinement, taking hostages) — **universal jurisdiction**; the non-renunciation of rights (7/7/7/8); the dissemination duty (47/48/127/144); the denunciation and Martens clauses. **GC I — wounded and sick in armed forces in the field** (64 articles): respect and protection of the wounded and sick, humane treatment and care without adverse distinction, the prohibition of murder, extermination, torture and biological experiments (Art 12); the search for and collection of the wounded and the dead after an engagement (15); recording and forwarding of information (16); the dead (17); the civilian population's role (18); medical units and establishments respected and protected (19-23); medical personnel, chaplains and the staff of relief societies respected and protected, and if captured retained only as needed and not as POWs (24-32); buildings and material (33-34); medical transports (35-37); the emblem — its use and protection (38-44). **GC II — wounded, sick and shipwrecked at sea** (63 articles): the same for naval warfare — hospital ships (22-35), which may not be attacked or captured, the shipwrecked, the medical personnel of ships. **GC III — prisoners of war** (below). **GC IV — civilians** (below)."));
c.push(H3("Prisoners of war — GC III"));
c.push(P("**Who is a POW (Art 4)**: persons who have fallen into the power of the enemy and are (1) members of the armed forces of a party and of militias or volunteer corps forming part of them; (2) members of other militias, volunteer corps and organised resistance movements belonging to a party, operating in or outside their own territory, provided that they (a) are commanded by a person responsible for his subordinates, (b) have a fixed distinctive sign recognisable at a distance, (c) carry arms openly, and (d) conduct their operations in accordance with the laws and customs of war — the four conditions; (3) members of regular armed forces professing allegiance to a Government not recognised by the Detaining Power; (4) persons accompanying the armed forces without being members (war correspondents, supply contractors, labour units) with identity cards; (5) crews of the merchant marine and civil aircraft; (6) the **levée en masse** — inhabitants of a non-occupied territory who on the approach of the enemy spontaneously take up arms, if they carry arms openly and respect the laws of war. AP I Art 44 relaxes the distinctive-sign and open-arms conditions for guerrillas (arms carried openly during each engagement and while visible to the adversary before an attack) — the reason the United States and India did not ratify. **Art 5** — where there is doubt whether a captured person is entitled to POW status, he enjoys the protection of the Convention until his status has been determined by a **competent tribunal** (the 'Art 5 tribunal' — the Guantánamo controversy; Hamdan v. Rumsfeld, 2006). Mercenaries (AP I Art 47) and spies (Art 46) are not entitled to POW status. **The regime**: **Art 12** — POWs are in the hands of the enemy Power, not of the individuals or units that captured them; the Detaining Power is responsible; **Art 13 — humane treatment**: POWs must at all times be humanely treated; any unlawful act or omission causing death or seriously endangering health is a serious breach; no physical mutilation or medical or scientific experiments; protection against acts of violence or intimidation and against **insults and public curiosity** (the 2019 Wing Commander Abhinandan video; the 1991 Gulf War parading of pilots); reprisals prohibited; **Art 14** — respect for their persons and honour; women treated with all the regard due to their sex and at least as favourably as men; **Art 15** — maintenance and medical attention free of charge; **Art 16** — equal treatment without adverse distinction; **Art 17** — the POW is bound to give only his surname, first names and rank, date of birth and serial number ('name, rank and number'); no physical or mental torture or coercion to secure information; those who refuse may not be threatened, insulted or exposed to unpleasant treatment; identity cards; **Arts 18-19** — personal effects; evacuation from the combat zone; **Arts 21-24** — internment (not close confinement except for penal or disciplinary sanctions; not in areas exposed to fire; not used to render points immune from military operations); **Arts 25-32** — quarters, food (sufficient to keep in good health; account taken of habitual diet), clothing, canteens, hygiene and medical attention (monthly medical inspections), the retained medical personnel; **Arts 34-38** — religion, intellectual and physical activities; **Art 39-42** — discipline, saluting, badges, weapons (only under extreme measures and after warnings); **Arts 43-45** — rank; **Arts 46-48** — transfer; **Arts 49-57 — labour**: officers may not be compelled to work; NCOs only supervisory; other POWs may be employed in work that is not military in character or purpose (agriculture, industry not connected with war, transport, commercial business, domestic service, public utility) and not unhealthy or dangerous unless volunteered (mine removal is dangerous); conditions equal to nationals; **Arts 58-68** — financial resources, pay (monthly advances by rank), working pay, accounts; **Arts 69-77** — relations with the exterior: notification of capture within a week (the capture card), correspondence (at least two letters and four cards a month), relief parcels, exemption from postal charges, legal documents; **Arts 78-81** — complaints to the military authorities, the Protecting Power and the ICRC; the **prisoners' representative** elected every six months; **Arts 82-108 — penal and disciplinary sanctions**: subject to the laws of the Detaining Power's armed forces; no collective punishment; escape is not an offence after successful escape and, if unsuccessful, only a disciplinary offence (Arts 91-93); disciplinary punishments limited (a fine, discontinuance of privileges, fatigue duties up to two hours daily, confinement — maximum thirty days); judicial proceedings only before military courts (or civil courts trying the Detaining Power's own forces) with the essential guarantees of independence and impartiality, notice to the Protecting Power, defence counsel, an interpreter, appeal; the death penalty only after the Protecting Power is notified and six months have passed (Arts 100-101); **Arts 109-117** — direct repatriation and accommodation in neutral countries of the seriously wounded and sick during hostilities; **Art 118** — POWs shall be **released and repatriated without delay after the cessation of active hostilities** (the 1971 war: 93,000 Pakistani POWs held until the Simla Agreement of 1972 and the Delhi Agreement of 1973 — India's position that repatriation awaited Pakistan's recognition of Bangladesh; Pakistan's ICJ case (Trial of Pakistani Prisoners of War, 1973) was withdrawn; Iran-Iraq, 1988-2003; the 'missing 54' Indian POWs of 1971 whom India claims Pakistan still holds); Art 119 — details; **Arts 120-121** — death and burial, inquiry into deaths; **Arts 122-125** — the **National Information Bureau** and the **Central Prisoners of War Information Agency** (the ICRC's Central Tracing Agency), relief societies; **Art 126** — the Protecting Power's and the ICRC's right to visit all places where POWs are, to interview them without witnesses, without limit on duration or frequency; **Arts 129-131** — grave breaches; universal jurisdiction. The Abhinandan case (27 February - 1 March 2019): India invoked Arts 13 and 17 against the videos of his interrogation; Pakistan repatriated him within sixty hours as a 'gesture of peace' — Art 118 was not yet engaged, as hostilities had not ceased in the Convention's sense, and the parties differed on whether an 'armed conflict' existed at all."));
c.push(H3("Civilians — GC IV and AP I"));
c.push(P("**GC IV** (159 articles) was the 1949 innovation — the first treaty devoted to civilians. **Part II (Arts 13-26)** — general protection of the whole population against certain consequences of war: hospital and safety zones, neutralised zones (14-15), the wounded, sick, infirm, expectant mothers (16), evacuation from besieged areas (17), civilian hospitals (18-20), transports (21-22), free passage of medical and religious consignments and of essential foodstuffs, clothing and tonics for children under fifteen, expectant mothers and maternity cases (23), children under fifteen orphaned or separated (24), family news (25), dispersed families (26). **Part III — protected persons** (Art 4): those who at a given moment and in any manner find themselves, in case of a conflict or occupation, in the hands of a party or Occupying Power of which they are not nationals (nationals of a co-belligerent or of a State with normal diplomatic relations are excluded — the Tadić judgment read 'nationality' as allegiance in ethnic conflicts); **Art 5** — the limited derogation for persons definitely suspected of hostile activity (but humane treatment and a fair trial remain); **Section I — common provisions (Arts 27-34)**: **Art 27** — protected persons are entitled in all circumstances to respect for their persons, honour, family rights, religious convictions and practices, manners and customs; humane treatment; protection against all acts of violence or threats, insults and public curiosity; **women shall be especially protected against any attack on their honour, in particular against rape, enforced prostitution, or any form of indecent assault**; no adverse distinction; the party may take such measures of control and security as may be necessary; Art 28 — no use as human shields; Art 29 — the party's responsibility; Art 30 — access to the Protecting Powers, the ICRC and relief societies; Art 31 — no physical or moral coercion, in particular to obtain information; Art 32 — no murder, torture, corporal punishment, mutilation, medical or scientific experiments, brutality; **Art 33** — no collective penalties, intimidation or terrorism; no pillage; **no reprisals** against protected persons or their property; **Art 34** — no hostages. **Section II — aliens in the territory of a party (Arts 35-46)**: the right to leave, treatment, employment, assigned residence and internment only if absolutely necessary, with review (41-43), refugees not to be treated as enemy aliens by reason only of their nationality (44), transfer only to a party to the Convention and never to a country where persecution is feared (45 — a non-refoulement rule). **Section III — occupied territories (Arts 47-78)**: the inviolability of rights (47); **Art 49** — individual or mass forcible transfers and deportations of protected persons from occupied territory are prohibited regardless of motive (evacuation for security or imperative military reasons excepted, with return), and **the Occupying Power shall not deport or transfer parts of its own civilian population into the territory it occupies** (the settlements provision — the Wall opinion, 2004; the ICJ's 2024 opinion on the Occupied Palestinian Territory); Art 50 — children, education; Art 51 — no compulsion to serve in the Occupying Power's forces; labour only for the occupying army's needs, public utilities or the population, paid, in the territory; Art 52 — workers' protection; **Art 53** — destruction of property prohibited except where rendered absolutely necessary by military operations; Art 54 — judges and public officials; Art 55 — the duty to ensure food and medical supplies; Art 56 — public health; Art 57 — hospitals; Art 58 — religion; Arts 59-63 — relief; **Arts 64-78** — penal legislation and procedure (the local law remains in force; the Occupying Power may legislate for security and orderly government; offences against its security may be punished but only after a regular trial, with the guarantees of Arts 71-75, the death penalty limited; **Art 78** — internment or assigned residence only for imperative reasons of security, with a right of appeal and six-monthly review). **Part III, Section IV — internees (Arts 79-135)** — a detailed regime parallel to the POW rules (places, food, clothing, hygiene, religion, property, administration, relations with the exterior, penal sanctions, transfer, death, release). **AP I (1977)** extends the protection of the civilian population in hostilities — Part IV: the basic rule of distinction (48), the definition of civilians (50 — in case of doubt, civilian), the general protection (51 — no attack, no terror, no indiscriminate attack, no reprisals, no shields), civilian objects (52), cultural objects and places of worship (53), **objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population** — starvation of civilians as a method of warfare prohibited; no attack on foodstuffs, crops, livestock, drinking water, irrigation (54), the natural environment (55), works and installations containing dangerous forces — dams, dykes, nuclear stations (56), precautions (57-58), non-defended localities and demilitarised zones (59-60), civil defence (61-67), relief (68-71), the treatment of persons in the power of a party — refugees and stateless persons (73), reunion of families (74), the **fundamental guarantees** (75 — the minimum for everyone: humane treatment, the prohibition of murder, torture, corporal punishment, mutilation, outrages upon personal dignity, hostages, collective punishments; the judicial guarantees), women (76), children (77 — no recruitment under fifteen; no death penalty under eighteen), evacuation of children (78), journalists (79 — civilians, with identity cards). **Occupation** is also governed by the Hague Regulations (Arts 42-56 — the occupant's authority, public order and civil life, the prohibition of forced allegiance, family honour and rights, private property, pillage, requisitions, public property as usufructuary) and, in practice, by the human-rights treaties which continue to apply (the Wall opinion)."));
c.push(H3("Common Art 3 and non-international armed conflict"));
c.push(P("**Common Art 3**: in the case of armed conflict not of an international character occurring in the territory of one of the parties, each party to the conflict (including the non-State armed group) is bound to apply as a minimum: (1) persons taking no active part in the hostilities, including members of armed forces who have laid down their arms and those placed hors de combat by sickness, wounds, detention or any other cause, shall in all circumstances be treated humanely, without any adverse distinction founded on race, colour, religion or faith, sex, birth or wealth, or any other similar criteria; to this end the following are and shall remain prohibited at any time and in any place whatsoever: (a) violence to life and person, in particular murder of all kinds, mutilation, cruel treatment and torture; (b) taking of hostages; (c) outrages upon personal dignity, in particular humiliating and degrading treatment; (d) the passing of sentences and the carrying out of executions without previous judgment pronounced by a regularly constituted court affording all the judicial guarantees recognised as indispensable by civilised peoples; (2) the wounded and sick shall be collected and cared for; an impartial humanitarian body such as the ICRC may offer its services; the parties should endeavour to bring the other provisions into force by special agreements; the application does not affect the legal status of the parties (the assurance that lets Governments accept it without recognising rebels). The ICJ in Nicaragua (1986) called Art 3 the 'minimum yardstick' applicable to all conflicts as customary law; the ICTY in Tadić (1995) defined armed conflict as 'protracted armed violence between governmental authorities and organised armed groups or between such groups within a State' — the two criteria of intensity and organisation that separate a NIAC from internal disturbances, riots and isolated acts of violence (to which IHL does not apply — AP II Art 1(2)). **AP II (1977)** — the first treaty devoted to NIACs (28 articles), with a higher threshold (Art 1(1): conflicts between a State's armed forces and dissident armed forces or other organised armed groups which, under responsible command, exercise such control over a part of the territory as to enable them to carry out sustained and concerted military operations and to implement the Protocol): fundamental guarantees (Art 4 — including the prohibition of rape, enforced prostitution and indecent assault, slavery, pillage, and the recruitment of children under fifteen), persons whose liberty is restricted (5), penal prosecutions (6 — including an amnesty at the end of hostilities for participation, 6(5)), the wounded and sick (7-12), the civilian population (13-18 — no attack, no starvation, no forced movement, protection of cultural objects, dams and relief). The Rome Statute's Art 8(2)(c) and (e) make serious violations of common Art 3 and of the laws of NIAC war crimes. **India** is not a party to AP II and treats its internal conflicts (Kashmir, the North-East, the Maoist areas) as law-and-order situations under the AFSPA and the ordinary law, not as armed conflicts — though common Art 3, being customary and part of the Geneva Conventions Act, applies whenever the Tadić threshold is met, and the ICRC's presence and the Human Rights Committee's 2024 observations treat them so."));
c.push(H3("Women and IHL"));
c.push(P("Women are protected as civilians and combatants under all the rules, and by **special provisions**: GC I Art 12 and GC II Art 12 (women treated 'with all consideration due to their sex'); GC III Art 14 (regard due to their sex; treatment at least as favourable as men), Art 25 (separate dormitories), Art 29 (separate sanitary conveniences), Art 88 (no more severe punishment; separate quarters and supervision by women), Arts 97 and 108; **GC IV Art 27(2)** — 'women shall be especially protected against any attack on their honour, in particular against rape, enforced prostitution, or any form of indecent assault' — the 1949 formulation in terms of 'honour', criticised for treating rape as a moral offence rather than violence; Arts 14, 16, 17, 23, 38(5), 50, 89(5), 91 and 132 (expectant mothers, maternity cases, mothers of children under seven — priority in relief, medical care, release from internment); Art 76 (women prisoners — separate quarters, supervision by women); Art 85 and 124 (internment); **AP I Art 76** — women shall be the object of special respect and shall be protected in particular against rape, forced prostitution and any other form of indecent assault; pregnant women and mothers of dependent infants — priority in release; no death penalty executed on them; Art 75(5); **AP II Art 4(2)(e)** — rape, enforced prostitution and indecent assault prohibited in NIACs; Art 5(2)(a) and 6(4). The **jurisprudence** transformed the 'honour' framing: **Akayesu (ICTR 1998)** — rape defined as 'a physical invasion of a sexual nature, committed on a person under circumstances which are coercive', and held to constitute genocide when committed with intent to destroy the group, and a crime against humanity; **Čelebići (ICTY 1998)** — rape as torture; **Furundžija (ICTY 1998)** — rape as a war crime and torture; forced nudity as an outrage on dignity; **Kunarac (ICTY 2001)** — the Foča case: rape and enslavement as crimes against humanity; the definition of rape without a requirement of force where coercive circumstances exist; sexual slavery; **Kvočka; Gacumbitsi**; the **Rome Statute's** Arts 7(1)(g) and 8(2)(b)(xxii)/(e)(vi) — rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy, enforced sterilisation and any other form of sexual violence of comparable gravity as crimes against humanity and war crimes; the ICC's **Bemba (2016, reversed 2018 on command responsibility)** and **Ntaganda (2019)** convictions for rape and sexual slavery, including of child soldiers within the perpetrator's own forces; the **Security Council's resolutions 1325 (2000), 1820 (2008 — sexual violence as a tactic of war and a threat to peace), 1888, 1889, 1960 (2010 — the monitoring and listing mechanism), 2106 (2013), 2122, 2242, 2467 (2019 — the survivor-centred approach)**; the **Special Representative on Sexual Violence in Conflict (2009)**; the **ICRC's 2001 study 'Women Facing War'** and the updated Commentaries' gender reading of 'honour'; the **CEDAW Committee's GR 30 (2013)** on women in conflict; and the **Nobel Peace Prize 2018** to Nadia Murad and Denis Mukwege. The Indian angle: the Bangladesh war's mass rapes (1971 — the 'birangona' — and the 1973 Bangladesh (Collaborators) and the 2010 International Crimes Tribunal), the Kunan Poshpora allegations (1991) and the AFSPA's Sec 6 sanction requirement, the Manipur violence of 2023, and the Verma Committee's (2013) recommendation to bring the sexual offences of the armed forces under the ordinary criminal law."));
c.push(H3("IHL and human rights law; India and IHL"));
c.push(P("**The relationship**: the two bodies are distinct in origin (Geneva and Hague vs the UN), scope (armed conflict vs all times), addressees (parties to the conflict including armed groups vs States), and enforcement (the Protecting Powers, the ICRC, grave-breach prosecutions vs the treaty bodies and courts), but converge: human-rights law continues to apply in armed conflict (the ICJ's Nuclear Weapons opinion, 1996 — the right to life applies, but what is 'arbitrary' is determined by IHL as the lex specialis; the Wall opinion, 2004 — three possibilities: some rights exclusively IHL, some exclusively human rights, some both; the DRC v. Uganda, 2005 — both applied to occupation; the Human Rights Committee's GC 31 and 36; the ECtHR's Hassan v. UK, 2014 — Art 5 read in the light of GC III and IV in Iraq), with derogation (Art 4 ICCPR, Art 15 ECHR) as the bridge; the Rome Statute's crimes against humanity apply in peace and war; the Council's 1993 Vienna Declaration and the 1968 Tehran Resolution XXIII ('human rights in armed conflicts') are the political recognition; the 'humanisation of humanitarian law' (Meron) — the Martens clause, common Art 3 and AP I Art 75 as the common floor. **India** ratified the Geneva Conventions on 9 November 1950 and enacted the **Geneva Conventions Act 1960**: **Sec 3** — punishment of grave breaches committed by any person within or outside India (death or life imprisonment for wilful killing; fourteen years for other grave breaches) — universal jurisdiction in Indian law, though never used; **Sec 4** — trial by court-martial or the ordinary courts, with the Central Government's sanction (Sec 17); **Secs 5-7** — notice of trial of protected persons to the Protecting Power, legal representation, appeals; **Secs 12-14** — protection of the red cross, red crescent and red lion and sun emblems and of the name 'Red Cross' (six months' imprisonment and fine); the four Conventions are Schedules to the Act. The Supreme Court in **Rev. Mons. Sebastiao Francisco Xavier dos Remedios Monteiro v. State of Goa (1969)** held that the Act does not give an individual a right to enforce the Conventions in the courts — the 1961 'liberation' of Goa was an act of State and the Conventions' occupation provisions ceased to apply on annexation; the Court's view that the Act creates offences but not private rights is the leading (and criticised) authority. India has not ratified the Protocols (its objections: AP I's Art 1(4) on wars of national liberation and Art 44's guerrilla rules; AP II's threshold and its application to internal conflicts), is a party to the Chemical Weapons Convention, the Biological Weapons Convention, the Conventional Weapons Convention and its Protocols (including the amended Protocol II on mines but not the Ottawa Convention — India retains anti-personnel mines on its borders), and is not a party to the Ottawa, Cluster Munitions or Nuclear Prohibition treaties; it applies IHL through the Army Act 1950, the Manual of Military Law and the Indian Armed Forces' training with the ICRC; the National Human Rights Commission and the courts (Naga People's Movement of Human Rights v. Union of India, 1998 — the AFSPA upheld with the 'dos and don'ts' for the forces, derived partly from IHL; Extra Judicial Execution Victim Families Association v. Union of India, 2016 — the 1,528 Manipur encounter deaths to be investigated; the CBI probes) apply human-rights law to internal security operations."));
c.push(CASE("Legality of the Threat or Use of Nuclear Weapons, ICJ Advisory Opinion 1996","Distinction and the prohibition of unnecessary suffering are the cardinal principles; IHL applies to nuclear weapons; human-rights law (the right to life) continues in war with IHL as lex specialis; the Court could not conclude definitively on the extreme circumstance of self-defence."));
c.push(CASE("Prosecutor v. Tadić (Jurisdiction), ICTY Appeals Chamber 1995","Armed conflict exists whenever there is a resort to armed force between States or protracted armed violence between governmental authorities and organised armed groups or between such groups; customary IHL applies in NIACs; individual criminal responsibility for violations of common Art 3."));
c.push(CASE("Legal Consequences of the Construction of a Wall in the Occupied Palestinian Territory, ICJ Advisory Opinion 2004","GC IV applies to the occupied territory; Art 49(6) prohibits the settlements; human-rights treaties apply extra-territorially in occupation; the wall violates IHL and human-rights law; all States must not recognise the illegal situation (common Art 1)."));
c.push(CASE("Prosecutor v. Kunarac, Kovač and Vuković, ICTY 2001-02","Rape and enslavement as crimes against humanity; the definition of rape turning on the absence of consent in coercive circumstances; sexual slavery; the Foča 'rape camps'."));
c.push(CASE("Rev. Mons. Sebastiao Francisco Xavier dos Remedios Monteiro v. State of Goa (1969) 3 SCC 419","The Geneva Conventions Act does not confer rights enforceable by individuals in Indian courts; occupation ended with annexation; the Conventions' occupation provisions ceased to apply — the leading (and criticised) Indian authority on IHL's domestic effect."));
c.push(CASE("Trial of Pakistani Prisoners of War (Pakistan v. India), ICJ 1973","Pakistan sought to prevent the transfer of 195 POWs to Bangladesh for trial; the case was discontinued after the Delhi Agreement; the episode is the standard illustration of GC III Art 118's repatriation duty and its political limits."));
c.push(DIG("The ICRC's customary-IHL database and the updated Commentaries are online; the 2017 Tallinn Manual 2.0 applies IHL to cyber operations (the ICRC's 2019 position paper; 2023 rules for civilian hackers); autonomous weapons are debated in the CCW's Group of Governmental Experts; satellite imagery and open-source investigation (Bellingcat, the UN's OSINT) document attacks on hospitals and schools; and the ICC's 2023-24 warrants for the Ukraine and Gaza conflicts rely on digital evidence under the Berkeley Protocol."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Geneva law and Hague law', 'Martens clause', 'Principle of distinction', 'Proportionality in IHL', 'Common Art 3', 'Grave breaches', 'Who is a prisoner of war (Art 4)', 'Name, rank and number (Art 17)', 'Repatriation of POWs (Art 118)', 'Protected persons (GC IV Art 4)', 'Art 49 GC IV', 'Women and IHL', 'Geneva Conventions Act 1960', 'Tadić definition of armed conflict'. Part B: 'Explain the basic principles of international humanitarian law and its sources'; or 'Discuss the rights of prisoners of war under the Third Geneva Convention'; or 'Discuss the protection of civilians under the Fourth Geneva Convention and Additional Protocol I'; or 'Examine the protection of women in armed conflict under IHL and the contribution of the international criminal tribunals'; or 'What is the relationship between IHL and human-rights law? Describe India's implementation of the Geneva Conventions'. Part C: a captured pilot is videotaped and questioned about his mission — GC III Arts 13 and 17, only name, rank, number; the video is a breach; an occupying army settles its civilians in the occupied territory — Art 49(6), the Wall opinion; an army destroys a village's wells and crops to deny insurgents food — AP I Art 54 / AP II Art 14, and customary law; a rebel group in a NIAC executes captured soldiers without trial — common Art 3(1)(d), a war crime under the Rome Statute Art 8(2)(c); a State says the Geneva Conventions do not apply because it has not declared war — common Art 2; a soldier rapes a civilian during operations — GC IV Art 27, AP I Art 76, Rome Statute Art 8, the Indian Army Act and the Verma Committee; India holds POWs after the ceasefire until a political settlement — Art 118 requires repatriation without delay after the cessation of active hostilities."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 11 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 11 — The Man in Pakistani Custody: Kulbhushan Jadhav, the ICJ, the ICC and the European Courts"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The International Court of Justice — jurisdiction, the human-rights cases (contentious and advisory), the limits · The ad hoc tribunals — ICTY, ICTR, the hybrid courts · The International Criminal Court — the Rome Statute's crimes, jurisdiction, triggers, complementarity, immunity, the cases; India's objections · The European Court of Human Rights and the Court of Justice of the EU as enforcement models (recap) · Universal jurisdiction and national courts"));
c.push(STORY("Islamabad, 10 April 2017. Pakistan announced that a Field General Court Martial had sentenced Kulbhushan Sudhir Jadhav, a retired Indian Navy officer whom it said it had arrested in Balochistan in March 2016, to death for espionage and terrorism on behalf of India's Research and Analysis Wing. Pakistan said he had confessed on video; India said he had been kidnapped from Iran, where he ran a business, and that the confession was coerced. What was not in dispute was that India had asked sixteen times for consular access under Article 36 of the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations — the right of a detained foreign national to have his consulate informed and to be visited by its officers — and had been refused each time, Pakistan taking the position that a spy has no such right and that a 2008 bilateral agreement excluded security cases. On 8 May 2017 India filed an application at the International Court of Justice, invoking the Optional Protocol to the Vienna Convention, and asked for provisional measures; on 18 May the Court unanimously ordered Pakistan to ensure that Jadhav was not executed pending the final decision. On 17 July 2019 the Court, fifteen votes to one (the Pakistani ad hoc judge dissenting), held that Pakistan had violated Article 36 — by not informing Jadhav of his rights, by not notifying India without delay, and by denying access — that espionage was no exception, that the bilateral agreement could not displace the Convention, and that Pakistan must provide 'effective review and reconsideration' of the conviction and sentence by means of its own choosing, taking full account of the violation, with the stay of execution as an indispensable condition. The Court did not order acquittal, release or a retrial, as India had asked; it applied its own precedents in LaGrand (Germany v. US, 2001) and Avena (Mexico v. US, 2004). Pakistan passed an ordinance in 2020, then an Act in 2021, to allow review by its High Court; India argues the review is not effective; Jadhav remains in Pakistani custody. The case is the Indian example of the ICJ as a human-rights court — a court of States, protecting an individual through the treaty rights of his State — and of the ICJ's limits."));
c.push(H3("The International Court of Justice"));
c.push(P("The principal judicial organ of the UN (Charter Art 92; the Statute annexed to the Charter, to which all UN members are parties — Art 93; fifteen judges elected for nine years by the General Assembly and the Security Council — Statute Arts 2-15; The Hague; the successor of the Permanent Court of International Justice, 1922-46). **Jurisdiction**: (1) **contentious** — only States may be parties (Art 34(1)); the Court has jurisdiction over cases the parties refer to it (special agreement — Art 36(1)), matters provided for in the Charter or in treaties in force (compromissory clauses — Art 36(1); the Genocide Convention's Art IX, the Torture Convention's Art 30, CERD's Art 22, CEDAW's Art 29, the Vienna Convention on Consular Relations' Optional Protocol — the routes for human-rights cases), and disputes between States that have made the **optional-clause declaration** accepting compulsory jurisdiction (Art 36(2) — 74 States; India's declaration of 2019 (replacing 1974) with its reservations excluding disputes with Commonwealth members, disputes on hostilities, disputes on multilateral treaties unless all parties are before the Court, and the self-judging domestic-jurisdiction reservation — the reason Pakistan's 1973 case and Jadhav went by treaty clauses); **provisional measures** (Art 41 — binding since LaGrand, 2001); judgments are final and binding on the parties (Arts 59-60), with recourse to the Security Council for non-compliance (Charter Art 94(2) — never used); (2) **advisory** — on any legal question at the request of the General Assembly, the Security Council or other authorised organs and agencies (Charter Art 96; Statute Art 65) — not binding but authoritative. **The human-rights cases**: **advisory** — Reservations to the Genocide Convention (1951 — a reservation incompatible with the object and purpose is impermissible; the Convention's principles bind even non-parties), Namibia (1971 — apartheid as a breach of the Charter), Western Sahara (1975 — self-determination), the Wall (2004), Kosovo (2010 — declarations of independence), Chagos (2019 — decolonisation and self-determination; the UK's separation of the Chagos Islands unlawful), the Occupied Palestinian Territory (2024 — the occupation unlawful, the settlements and the discriminatory regime violate IHL and CERD), the Climate Change opinion (2025); **contentious** — Corfu Channel (1949 — 'elementary considerations of humanity'), Barcelona Traction (1970 — erga omnes), Tehran Hostages (1980), Nicaragua (1986 — common Art 3 as customary law; force not a means of protecting human rights), East Timor (1995 — self-determination erga omnes but the Monetary Gold rule), **Bosnia v. Serbia (2007)** — the first genocide judgment: Srebrenica was genocide, Serbia not responsible for committing it but responsible for failing to prevent and to punish, the duty to prevent triggered by knowledge of a serious risk; Croatia v. Serbia (2015 — no genocide proved either way); DRC v. Uganda (2005 — occupation, IHL and human rights; reparations 2022, US$325 million); Diallo (Guinea v. DRC, 2010 — the first judgment on the ICCPR's Arts 9 and 13 in a diplomatic-protection case, with compensation in 2012); **LaGrand (2001) and Avena (2004)** — consular access under Art 36 VCCR as an individual right; Belgium v. Senegal (2012 — the duty to prosecute Habré; torture prohibition as jus cogens); Germany v. Italy (2012 — State immunity prevails over civil claims for war crimes — the limit); **Jadhav (2019)**; Georgia v. Russia (2011 — CERD's procedural preconditions); Ukraine v. Russia (2019-24 — CERD and terrorism financing; and the 2022 case under the Genocide Convention with provisional measures ordering Russia to suspend its invasion); Qatar v. UAE (2021 — CERD's 'national origin' does not cover current nationality); **The Gambia v. Myanmar (2020, 2022)** — the Rohingya genocide case, brought by a non-injured State (erga omnes partes), with provisional measures; Armenia v. Azerbaijan and Azerbaijan v. Armenia (2021-, CERD); **South Africa v. Israel (2024)** — provisional measures on Gaza under the Genocide Convention (Israel to prevent genocidal acts, ensure humanitarian aid, preserve evidence; three further orders); Nicaragua v. Germany (2024 — arms transfers; no measures); Canada and the Netherlands v. Syria (2023 — torture; provisional measures). **The limits**: only States have standing (the individual has no direct access — the diplomatic-protection filter); consent-based jurisdiction (the reservations, the Monetary Gold rule); the political control of enforcement (Art 94(2) and the veto — Nicaragua's judgment was never enforced against the United States; Russia ignores the 2022 order); the 'effective review' remedy short of release (Jadhav; Avena — Mexico's nationals were executed regardless, Medellín v. Texas, 2008); the slow pace; and the Court's unwillingness to find responsibility for genocide on circumstantial evidence (the 'fully conclusive' standard of Bosnia). Its strengths: authoritative declarations (Namibia, the Wall, Chagos) that shape the political process; the provisional-measures practice since 2020 (Myanmar, Ukraine, Gaza) that has made the Court a forum of first resort for atrocity situations; and the recognition of erga omnes partes standing that lets any party to the Genocide, Torture and Racial Discrimination Conventions sue."));
c.push(H3("The ad hoc tribunals and the hybrid courts"));
c.push(P("The Security Council, acting under Chapter VII, created the **International Criminal Tribunal for the former Yugoslavia** (Resolution 827, 1993; The Hague; 161 indicted, 90 convicted — Tadić, Krstić (Srebrenica genocide), Kunarac, Milošević (died 2006), Karadžić (life, 2016-19), Mladić (life, 2017-21); closed 2017) and the **International Criminal Tribunal for Rwanda** (Resolution 955, 1994; Arusha; 93 indicted, 62 convicted — Akayesu, Kambanda (the first head of government to plead guilty to genocide), the Media case (Nahimana — the Radio Mille Collines incitement); closed 2015), with the **International Residual Mechanism (2010)** continuing their functions; the Tadić appeal (1995) upheld the Council's power to create them. Their contributions: the definitions of genocide, crimes against humanity and war crimes; rape as genocide and torture; joint criminal enterprise and command responsibility (Čelebići); the application of IHL to NIACs; the rules of procedure and evidence that the ICC inherited; the precedent for prosecuting sitting and former heads of State. The **hybrid (internationalised) courts** — the **Special Court for Sierra Leone** (2002; Charles Taylor convicted 2012 — the first head of State convicted since Nuremberg; child soldiers; forced marriage), the **Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia** (2006; the Khmer Rouge — Duch, Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan), the **Special Tribunal for Lebanon** (2009; the Hariri assassination), the East Timor Special Panels, the Kosovo Specialist Chambers (2016 — Thaçi), the Bosnian War Crimes Chamber, the Central African Republic's Special Criminal Court, and **Bangladesh's International Crimes Tribunal (2010)** for the 1971 atrocities — a domestic court criticised for its fair-trial standards, and, from 2024, applied to the ousted Awami League leadership. The **UN's investigative mechanisms** — the IIIM for Syria (2016), the IIMM for Myanmar (2018), UNITAD for ISIL crimes in Iraq (2017) — collect evidence for future prosecutions where no court has jurisdiction."));
c.push(H3("The International Criminal Court"));
c.push(P("The **Rome Statute** was adopted on 17 July 1998 (120 for, 7 against — the United States, China, Israel, Iraq, Libya, Qatar, Yemen — 21 abstentions including India) and entered into force on 1 July 2002; 125 parties (2025 — Burundi (2017), the Philippines (2019) and Hungary (2025) withdrew; Ukraine joined 2025; the United States, China, Russia (which withdrew its signature in 2016), India, Pakistan, Israel, Turkey, Iran and most Arab States are outside). The Court sits at The Hague with eighteen judges (Pre-Trial, Trial and Appeals Divisions), the Prosecutor (Luis Moreno Ocampo 2003-12, Fatou Bensouda 2012-21, Karim Khan 2021-), the Registry, the Assembly of States Parties, and the Trust Fund for Victims. **The crimes (Art 5)**: **genocide (Art 6)** — the 1948 Convention's definition (acts committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnical, racial or religious group, as such — killing, serious bodily or mental harm, conditions of life calculated to destroy, preventing births, transferring children); **crimes against humanity (Art 7)** — murder, extermination, enslavement, deportation or forcible transfer, imprisonment, torture, rape and the sexual crimes, persecution on political, racial, national, ethnic, cultural, religious, gender or other grounds, enforced disappearance, apartheid, other inhumane acts — when committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against any civilian population, with knowledge of the attack (no nexus with armed conflict required); **war crimes (Art 8)** — grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions (8(2)(a)), other serious violations of the laws and customs of IAC (8(2)(b) — 26 acts including attacks on civilians and civilian objects, disproportionate attacks, attacks on undefended places, killing the hors de combat, perfidy, the transfer of population into occupied territory, attacks on cultural property, pillage, poison and gas, expanding bullets, outrages on dignity, rape and sexual violence, using human shields, starvation, conscripting children under fifteen), serious violations of common Art 3 (8(2)(c)) and other serious violations of the laws of NIAC (8(2)(e)); the 2010 Kampala amendments added weapons crimes in NIAC and **the crime of aggression (Art 8 bis)** — the planning, preparation, initiation or execution, by a person in a position to control the political or military action of a State, of an act of aggression which by its character, gravity and scale constitutes a manifest violation of the Charter — activated in 2018 but with jurisdiction only over parties that have accepted the amendment (Art 15 bis; hence the proposals for a special tribunal for aggression against Ukraine). **Jurisdiction**: **Art 11** — crimes after 1 July 2002 (temporal); **Art 12** — preconditions: the State on whose territory the crime occurred or the State of the accused's nationality is a party or has accepted jurisdiction ad hoc (Art 12(3) — Palestine 2015, Ukraine 2014-15); **Art 13 — the triggers**: referral by a State party (Uganda, DRC, CAR, Mali — the 'self-referrals'; the 2018 referral of Venezuela by six States; Ukraine by 43 States in 2022; Palestine by South Africa and others in 2023-24), referral by the **Security Council under Chapter VII** regardless of the State's status (Darfur 2005 — Resolution 1593; Libya 2011 — Resolution 1970; the Syria referral vetoed in 2014), and the **Prosecutor's own initiative (proprio motu — Art 15)** with Pre-Trial Chamber authorisation (Kenya 2010, Côte d'Ivoire 2011, Georgia 2016, Burundi 2017, Afghanistan 2020, Bangladesh/Myanmar 2019 — the Rohingya deportation into a State party, the Philippines 2021); **Art 16** — deferral by the Council for twelve months (the US-sought Resolutions 1422 and 1487 of 2002-03); **Art 17 — complementarity**: the Court is a court of last resort — a case is inadmissible where it is being investigated or prosecuted by a State with jurisdiction unless the State is **unwilling or unable genuinely** to carry out the investigation or prosecution (the shielding of the person, unjustified delay, lack of independence; a total or substantial collapse of the national system); and where the case is not of sufficient gravity; **Art 25** — individual criminal responsibility (commission, ordering, aiding, contributing to a group crime, incitement to genocide, attempt); **Art 27** — official capacity is irrelevant: heads of State and Government, members of Governments and Parliaments enjoy no immunity before the Court; **Art 28** — command and superior responsibility; **Art 33** — superior orders no defence unless the order was not manifestly unlawful (orders to commit genocide or crimes against humanity are manifestly unlawful); **Art 98** — the Court may not request surrender that would require a State to act inconsistently with its obligations of immunity to a third State (the Al Bashir controversy — the Appeals Chamber in 2019 held that there is no head-of-State immunity before an international court under customary law, so Jordan and South Africa had a duty to arrest; the Mongolia non-compliance finding of 2024 over Putin); **Part 6-8** — trial (the rights of the accused — Art 67; victims' participation and reparations — Arts 68, 75; the Trust Fund), penalties (up to thirty years or life; no death penalty — Art 77), appeal; **Part 9** — cooperation and surrender (the parties' duty; the Court has no police); **Art 124** (the seven-year war-crimes opt-out, deleted 2015); **Art 127** — withdrawal. **The cases**: **Lubanga (2012)** — the first conviction: conscripting and using child soldiers in the DRC, fourteen years; Katanga (2014); **Bemba (2016 — the first conviction for sexual violence and for command responsibility; acquitted on appeal 2018)**; **Al Mahdi (2016)** — the destruction of Timbuktu's mausoleums as a war crime; **Ntaganda (2019)** — thirty years, including sexual crimes against his own child soldiers; **Ongwen (2021)** — 25 years, the former child soldier turned commander, forced marriage as an inhumane act; **Al Hassan (2024)**; the acquittals — Gbagbo (2019), the Kenyan cases collapsed (Kenyatta 2014, Ruto 2016 — witness interference); the **warrants** — Al Bashir (2009, 2010 — genocide; never surrendered; Sudan's 2020 agreement to hand him over unfulfilled), Gaddafi (2011), Saif al-Islam, **Putin and Lvova-Belova (17 March 2023 — the deportation of Ukrainian children)**, the Russian commanders (2024), **Netanyahu and Gallant (21 November 2024 — starvation as a method of warfare and crimes against humanity in Gaza; the Hamas leaders' warrants withdrawn on their deaths)**, the Taliban leaders (2025), Duterte (arrested and surrendered by the Philippines in March 2025). **The criticisms**: the African focus of the first decade (all first cases African — the AU's 2016 withdrawal strategy), the great powers' immunity in practice (no Council referral of Syria; the US sanctions on the Prosecutor in 2020 and 2025), the slow and expensive proceedings, the low conviction rate, the dependence on State cooperation, and the aggression amendment's narrow reach. **India's position**: India abstained at Rome and has not signed, objecting to (1) the Council's referral and deferral powers (Arts 13(b) and 16), which subject non-parties to the Court through the veto-holders' politics; (2) the exclusion of nuclear weapons (and terrorism) from the list of crimes despite India's proposal; (3) the definition of war crimes in NIAC as an intrusion into internal affairs (Kashmir, the North-East) and the inclusion of the transfer of population; (4) the proprio motu power of the Prosecutor; (5) the principle of complementarity as inadequate protection; (6) the treaty's application to non-parties' nationals through territorial jurisdiction; and, more generally, sovereignty and the armed forces' exposure. India has enacted no statute on crimes against humanity or genocide (it ratified the Genocide Convention in 1959 without implementing legislation — the Law Commission's 2018 consultation and the 2019 Bills lapsed), so those crimes are prosecuted, if at all, as ordinary offences — the 1984, 2002 and 2020 mass violence being the standing examples."));
c.push(H3("The European courts as enforcement — recap; universal jurisdiction"));
c.push(P("The **European Court of Human Rights** (Episode 05) is the model of an enforcement court for human rights: compulsory jurisdiction over individual applications, binding judgments, supervised execution — the only system in which the individual has a direct, enforceable international remedy; its 'European Court of Justice' namesake, the **CJEU** (Episode 05), enforces the EU's Charter through the preliminary-reference procedure, annulment and infringement actions, and the rule-of-law conditionality — an enforcement of human rights within a supranational legal order rather than a human-rights court as such; the syllabus's 'role of the European Court of Justice' is answered by the Kadi line (fundamental rights as limits on the EU's and the UN's measures), the data-protection cases, and the judicial-independence cases against Poland and Hungary (Commission v. Poland, 2019, 2021, 2023 — the disciplinary chamber; the €1 million daily penalty; the rule-of-law mechanism). **National courts and universal jurisdiction** complete the picture: the Geneva Conventions' grave-breaches regime, the Torture Convention's Art 5(2) and 7 (prosecute or extradite — **Pinochet (No 3), House of Lords 1999** — no immunity of a former head of State for torture after the Convention's ratification), the Belgian and Spanish universal-jurisdiction laws (narrowed after the Yerodia case, Arrest Warrant (DRC v. Belgium), ICJ 2002 — a sitting foreign minister's immunity from national courts), the German Koblenz trial (2022 — the first conviction of Syrian State torture, under the Code of Crimes against International Law), the French, Swedish and Dutch prosecutions of Syrian and Rwandan perpetrators, the US Alien Tort Statute (Filartiga; narrowed by Kiobel, 2013, and Nestlé, 2021), and India's Geneva Conventions Act Sec 3 (unused) — the 'horizontal' enforcement that the ICC's complementarity presupposes."));
c.push(CASE("Jadhav (India v. Pakistan), ICJ 2019","Pakistan violated Art 36 VCCR by denying consular access; espionage is no exception; the bilateral agreement does not displace the Convention; the remedy is effective review and reconsideration of the conviction and sentence, with a continued stay of execution."));
c.push(CASE("LaGrand (Germany v. United States), ICJ 2001; Avena (Mexico v. United States), ICJ 2004","Art 36 VCCR creates individual rights; provisional measures are binding; the remedy for a violation is review and reconsideration of the conviction and sentence by the judicial process, not the executive's clemency alone."));
c.push(CASE("Bosnia and Herzegovina v. Serbia and Montenegro, ICJ 2007","Srebrenica was genocide; the duty to prevent under Art I is one of conduct, due diligence, triggered by knowledge of a serious risk; Serbia breached it and the duty to punish; the 'fully conclusive' evidentiary standard for attribution."));
c.push(CASE("Arrest Warrant of 11 April 2000 (DRC v. Belgium), ICJ 2002","A serving foreign minister enjoys full immunity from foreign national criminal jurisdiction even for war crimes and crimes against humanity; immunity is not impunity — trial at home, waiver, after leaving office, or before an international court."));
c.push(CASE("Prosecutor v. Lubanga, ICC 2012","The first ICC conviction — enlisting and conscripting children under fifteen and using them to participate actively in hostilities; the Court's approach to child soldiers, victims' participation and reparations."));
c.push(CASE("Prosecutor v. Al Bashir (Jordan referral), ICC Appeals Chamber 2019","No head-of-State immunity before an international court under customary international law; States parties must arrest and surrender a sitting head of State subject to an ICC warrant; the Art 98 argument rejected."));
c.push(CASE("R v. Bow Street Magistrate, ex parte Pinochet (No 3), House of Lords 1999","A former head of State has no immunity from extradition proceedings for torture committed after the Torture Convention bound the States concerned; the Convention's universal-jurisdiction scheme is incompatible with immunity for the very acts it criminalises."));
c.push(DIG("The ICJ's hearings are webcast and its case files online; the ICC's Project Harmony and the OTPLink portal receive digital evidence; the Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations (2020) sets the evidentiary standard; the Putin warrant rests on satellite and social-media evidence of the deportations; and the 2025 US sanctions on the Prosecutor and judges — enforced through payment and cloud providers — are the digital-era pressure on the Court."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Jurisdiction of the ICJ', 'Optional clause (Art 36(2))', 'Advisory jurisdiction', 'Jadhav case', 'Provisional measures', 'Bosnia v. Serbia', 'ICTY and ICTR', 'Crimes under the Rome Statute', 'Complementarity', 'Art 27 Rome Statute', 'Triggers of ICC jurisdiction', 'India and the ICC', 'Universal jurisdiction', 'Pinochet case'. Part B: 'Examine the role of the International Court of Justice in the enforcement of human rights, with its limitations'; or 'Describe the jurisdiction and functioning of the International Criminal Court and evaluate India's objections'; or 'Discuss the role of the European Court of Human Rights and the Court of Justice of the European Union in the enforcement of human rights'; or 'Write a note on the ad hoc tribunals'. Part C: a State's national is sentenced to death abroad after being denied consular access — the VCCR's Optional Protocol, LaGrand/Avena/Jadhav, provisional measures, review and reconsideration; a non-party State's army commits crimes against humanity on the territory of a State party — Art 12(2)(a), the Court has jurisdiction (Bangladesh/Myanmar; Ukraine before 2025); a sitting head of State of a non-party is charged — Art 27 and Al Bashir, but arrest depends on parties' cooperation (Putin in Mongolia); a State party is investigating the same crimes — Art 17, admissible only if unwilling or unable; a torturer visits a Torture Convention party — Art 5(2), 7, Pinochet, the Koblenz precedent; a State ignores an ICJ provisional order — Art 94(2), the Council, and the political consequences (Russia 2022)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 12 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 12 — Hanuffa Khatoon at Howrah Station: The Constitution, the Courts and the Protection of Human Rights Act 1993"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The constitutional mechanism — the Preamble, Parts III and IV, Arts 32 and 226, Art 51(c), Art 253, the incorporation doctrine and the cases · Public interest litigation and the human-rights jurisprudence of Art 21 · The Protection of Human Rights Act 1993 — the NHRC, the SHRCs, the Human Rights Courts, the 2006 and 2019 amendments, the Paris Principles · The other national institutions · An assessment"));
c.push(STORY("Howrah Station, Calcutta, 26 February 1998. Hanuffa Khatoon, a member of the Union Board of a village in Bangladesh, arrived at Howrah on her way to Ajmer and found her train to Delhi delayed. A tout in the uniform of a railway employee offered her a room in the Yatri Niwas, the railway's lodging for passengers; there, two railway men and their friends plied her with liquor and raped her; the next day another railway employee took her to his rented flat with a promise of help, where she was raped again. She escaped and was rescued by the police. Chandrima Das, a Calcutta advocate, filed a writ petition under Article 226 in the Calcutta High Court, which awarded Hanuffa Khatoon ten lakh rupees as compensation against the Union of India, the Railway Board's employer. The Railways appealed to the Supreme Court on two grounds: that a foreigner has no fundamental rights and no claim under Article 21, and that the employer is not vicariously liable for its employees' crimes, so that her remedy was a civil suit for damages. Justice Saghir Ahmad rejected both. The right to life under Article 21 belongs to every person, not only to citizens, and its content includes dignity — the Court read the Universal Declaration's Articles 1, 2, 3, 5, 7 and 9 into it through Article 51(c) and said that international covenants and declarations adopted by the United Nations must be respected by all signatory States in a manner that gives them effect. The railway employees were the State's servants acting in the course of the State's commercial function; the State's liability under the public-law remedy of Article 32 and 226 is not the tort law of vicarious liability but the constitutional liability for the violation of a fundamental right, for which compensation is the appropriate relief (Nilabati Behera). The award stood. The case is the meeting point of this episode's three themes: the Constitution's rights, the courts' remedies, and India's international obligations — and it shows why the Protection of Human Rights Act, passed five years earlier, defines human rights by reference to both the Constitution and the Covenants."));
c.push(H3("The constitutional mechanism"));
c.push(P("**The Preamble** — justice (social, economic, political), liberty (thought, expression, belief, faith, worship), equality (status, opportunity), fraternity (the dignity of the individual and the unity of the nation) — the UDHR's values in the Constituent Assembly's language (drafted 1947-49 alongside the Declaration; the Fundamental Rights Sub-Committee under Sardar Patel, with K.M. Munshi and B.R. Ambedkar). **Part III — the Fundamental Rights**: equality (Arts 14-18 — equality before law, non-discrimination, equality of opportunity, abolition of untouchability and titles); freedoms (Art 19 — speech, assembly, association, movement, residence, profession; Arts 20-22 — protection in conviction, life and personal liberty, protection against arrest and detention); against exploitation (Arts 23-24 — trafficking, forced labour, child labour); religion (Arts 25-28); cultural and educational rights of minorities (Arts 29-30); the right to constitutional remedies (Art 32 — the Supreme Court's writ jurisdiction, 'the heart and soul of the Constitution' (Ambedkar); Art 226 — the High Courts', wider); Art 21A (education, 2002); the derogation provisions (Art 33 — the armed forces; Art 34 — martial law; Arts 358-359 — emergency, as narrowed by the 44th Amendment so that Arts 20-21 can never be suspended, the lesson of ADM Jabalpur (1976), overruled in Puttaswamy (2017)). **Part IV — the Directive Principles** — the ICESCR's rights as non-justiciable directives: adequate means of livelihood, equal pay, the health of workers and children (Art 39), equal justice and legal aid (39A), village panchayats (40), work, education and public assistance (41), just and humane conditions of work and maternity relief (42), a living wage (43), workers' participation (43A), a uniform civil code (44), early childhood care (45), the weaker sections (46), nutrition and health (47), agriculture and animal husbandry (48), the environment (48A), monuments (49), the separation of the judiciary (50), and **Art 51 — the promotion of international peace and security: (a) international peace; (b) just and honourable relations; (c) 'foster respect for international law and treaty obligations in the dealings of organised peoples with one another'; (d) arbitration** — the provision through which the courts read the international instruments; **Part IVA — Art 51A — the Fundamental Duties** (1976) — the citizen's side of the African Charter's idea. **The treaty-making and incorporation provisions**: Art 73 (the executive's power extends to matters on which Parliament may legislate — treaties are made by the executive), **Art 253** (Parliament may make any law for implementing any treaty, agreement or convention, notwithstanding the distribution of powers — the basis of the Protection of Human Rights Act, the Geneva Conventions Act, the Environment (Protection) Act, the Anti-Hijacking Act, the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act), Entry 14 of List I (treaties and their implementation), Art 246 — so that India is **dualist**: a treaty binds India internationally on ratification but becomes domestic law only by legislation, except that (a) customary international law is part of the law of the land unless contrary to statute (the common-law rule adopted in Gramophone Co v. Birendra Bahadur Pandey, 1984 — 'the comity of nations requires that rules of international law may be accommodated in the municipal law even without express legislative sanction provided they do not run into conflict with Acts of Parliament'), (b) statutes are interpreted in conformity with international obligations where possible, and (c) the fundamental rights are interpreted in the light of the international instruments where they are not inconsistent (**Vishaka**, 1997 — 'any international convention not inconsistent with the fundamental rights and in harmony with its spirit must be read into these provisions to enlarge the meaning and content thereof, to promote the object of the constitutional guarantee'; Apparel Export; Chandrima Das; PUCL (telephone tapping); **Puttaswamy**, 2017 — Art 17 ICCPR and Art 12 UDHR; **Navtej Johar**; **Kaushal Kishor**, 2023). The **cases on Art 21's expansion** are the human-rights jurisprudence: Maneka Gandhi (1978 — 'procedure established by law' must be fair, just and reasonable — the due-process reading), Francis Coralie Mullin (1981 — life with dignity), Olga Tellis (1985 — livelihood), M.C. Mehta (1987 — environment), Bandhua Mukti Morcha (1984), Sunil Batra (1978-80 — prisoners' rights), Hussainara Khatoon (1979 — speedy trial and legal aid; the undertrials), Sheela Barse (1983 — women in custody), Rudul Sah (1983 — compensation for illegal detention), Nilabati Behera (1993), D.K. Basu (1997 — the eleven arrest guidelines, now in the CrPC/BNSS), Vishaka, Chandrima Das, Paschim Banga Khet Mazdoor Samity (1996 — emergency medical care), Unni Krishnan (1993 — education), PUCL (2001 — food), Selvi (2010 — narco-analysis), Shatrughan Chauhan (2014 — delay in mercy petitions as a ground for commutation), NALSA (2014), Puttaswamy (2017), Common Cause (2018 — passive euthanasia and the living will), Navtej Johar (2018), Joseph Shine (2018), Anuradha Bhasin (2020), and the 2023-24 rulings on bail (Satender Kumar Antil) and the UAPA."));
c.push(H3("Public interest litigation and the courts' human-rights role"));
c.push(P("From 1979 (Hussainara Khatoon; the Bhagwati-Krishna Iyer years) the Supreme Court relaxed **locus standi** — any member of the public acting bona fide may move the Court for the enforcement of the rights of persons unable to do so themselves (S.P. Gupta, 1981); accepted **letters as petitions** (the 'epistolary jurisdiction' — Sunil Batra's postcard; Bandhua Mukti Morcha's letter); appointed **commissions** to investigate facts (Bandhua Mukti Morcha; Sheela Barse); issued **continuing mandamus** and monitored implementation (Vineet Narain, 1998; the 2G, coal and Manipur cases); laid down **guidelines** to fill legislative gaps (Vishaka; D.K. Basu; the 2006 Prakash Singh police reforms; the 2011-14 Lalita Kumari directions); awarded **compensation** as a public-law remedy (Rudul Sah; Nilabati Behera; Chandrima Das); and treated the Directive Principles and the international instruments as sources of Art 21's content. The **criticisms**: judicial overreach and the 'Government by judiciary' debate; the inconsistency of relief; the Court's own retreats (the 2010-20 caution in Divisional Manager, Aravali Golf Club, and the 2020s' bail and sedition decisions); the delay and the pendency; the limited follow-through on structural orders. But the PIL is the mechanism through which the constitutional protection of human rights has been made real for those without access — bonded labourers, undertrials, the homeless, custodial victims, the displaced — and it is the model that the South Asian and South African courts adopted."));
c.push(H3("The Protection of Human Rights Act 1993"));
c.push(P("Enacted after the Vienna Conference and the **Paris Principles** (the 1991 Principles relating to the Status of National Institutions, endorsed by the General Assembly in 1993 — independence, a broad mandate, pluralism, adequate resources, cooperation with NGOs), and after international criticism of India's record in Punjab and Kashmir (the Amnesty reports; the US State Department's; the Government's wish to have a domestic body to answer them), by the **Protection of Human Rights Ordinance of 28 September 1993** and the Act of 8 January 1994, deemed in force from the ordinance; amended in **2006** (the NHRC's powers over the SHRCs, the deemed-member Chairpersons of the Commissions for Minorities, SCs, STs and Women, the SHRCs' composition, the transfer of complaints, the 'Human Rights Courts') and in **2019** (the Chairperson may be any former Supreme Court judge, not only a former Chief Justice; the tenure reduced from five to three years; the Commissions for Backward Classes, for the Protection of Child Rights and the Chief Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities as deemed members; a woman member; the SHRC Chairperson may be any former High Court judge; the Union Territories' complaints to the NHRC). **Chapter I — Sec 2 definitions**: **(d) 'human rights'** — the rights relating to life, liberty, equality and dignity of the individual guaranteed by the Constitution or embodied in the International Covenants and enforceable by courts in India; **(f) 'International Covenants'** — the ICCPR and the ICESCR of 16 December 1966 and such other Covenant or Convention adopted by the General Assembly as the Central Government may specify by notification (no further Convention has been notified, although the NHRC has recommended notifying CEDAW and the CRC, and their standards reach the Commission through the definition's reference to the Constitution and the courts' incorporation cases); (g) 'Member'; (h) 'National Human Rights Commission'; (i) 'Public servant' (Sec 21 IPC). **Chapter II — the NHRC**: **Sec 3** — constitution: a **Chairperson** who has been a Chief Justice of India or a Judge of the Supreme Court (2019); one Member who is or has been a Judge of the Supreme Court; one Member who is or has been a Chief Justice of a High Court; three Members (at least one a woman) to be appointed from among persons having knowledge of or practical experience in matters relating to human rights (2019 — raised from two to three); the **deemed Members** — the Chairpersons of the National Commissions for Minorities, SCs, STs, Women, Backward Classes and the Protection of Child Rights, and the Chief Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities (for the functions under Sec 12(b)-(j)); a Secretary-General as the chief executive; headquarters at Delhi; **Sec 4** — appointment by the President on the recommendation of a committee of the Prime Minister (chair), the Speaker, the Home Minister, the Leaders of the Opposition in both Houses and the Deputy Chairman of the Rajya Sabha; a sitting judge only after consultation with the Chief Justice of India; **Sec 5** — removal by the President on proved misbehaviour or incapacity after a Supreme Court inquiry, or for insolvency, paid employment, infirmity, unsound mind, conviction; **Sec 6** — term: three years (2019) or age seventy, eligible for reappointment; no further Government employment except the SHRC; **Secs 7-8** — vacancies, procedure; **Sec 9** — acts not invalid by vacancy; **Sec 10** — procedure regulated by the Commission; **Sec 11** — officers and staff (an Investigation Division under a Director General of Police — the source of the criticism that the NHRC's investigators are serving police officers). **Chapter III — functions and powers**: **Sec 12** — the Commission shall (a) inquire, suo motu or on a petition presented by a victim or any person on his behalf (or on a court's order — 2006), into complaints of (i) violation of human rights or abetment thereof, or (ii) **negligence in the prevention of such violation by a public servant** (the formula that brings private violence within the Commission's reach through the State's failure); (b) intervene in any proceeding involving any allegation of violation of human rights pending before a court with the court's approval; (c) **visit**, notwithstanding anything in any other law, any jail or other institution under the State Government's control where persons are detained or lodged for treatment, reformation or protection, to study the inmates' living conditions and make recommendations (2006 removed the requirement of intimation to the State); (d) review the safeguards provided by the Constitution or any law and recommend measures for their effective implementation; (e) review the factors, including acts of terrorism, that inhibit the enjoyment of human rights and recommend remedial measures; (f) study treaties and other international instruments and make recommendations for their effective implementation; (g) undertake and promote research; (h) spread human-rights literacy and promote awareness of the safeguards through publications, the media, seminars; (i) encourage the efforts of NGOs and institutions; (j) such other functions as it may consider necessary. **Sec 13** — powers relating to inquiries: the powers of a civil court trying a suit (summoning and enforcing attendance and examining on oath, discovery and production of documents, evidence on affidavits, requisitioning public records, commissions for witnesses), the power to require information from any person (Sec 13(2), with the protection of the Indian Penal Code's Sec 176-177), to enter and search any building and seize documents (13(3)), and the inquiry is a judicial proceeding for Secs 193 and 228 IPC (13(4)-(5)); the 2006 amendment added the power to transfer complaints to the SHRC (Sec 13(6)) and to take over a complaint pending with an SHRC (Sec 36(1) reading). **Sec 14** — investigation: the Commission may use the services of any officer or investigation agency of the Central or State Government, which must report; the Commission may examine witnesses. **Sec 15** — a statement made to the Commission in an inquiry cannot be used against the maker in civil or criminal proceedings except for perjury. **Sec 16** — persons likely to be prejudicially affected to be heard. **Chapter IV — procedure**: **Sec 17** — on receiving a complaint the Commission may call for information or a report from the Government within a time; if no report, it may inquire on its own; if the report shows no further inquiry is required, or that action has been taken, it may close the matter; **Sec 18 — steps after inquiry**: where the inquiry discloses a violation or negligence, the Commission may (a) **recommend** to the Government or authority (i) to make payment of compensation or damages to the complainant or the victim or his family, (ii) to initiate proceedings for prosecution or such other suitable action against the person(s) concerned, (iii) such further action as it thinks fit; (b) **approach the Supreme Court or the High Court** for such directions, orders or writs as the court may deem necessary; (c) recommend the grant of **immediate interim relief** to the victim or his family; (d) subject to (e), provide a copy of the inquiry report to the petitioner; (e) send a copy of the report with its recommendations to the Government or authority, which shall within **one month** (or such further time as the Commission allows) forward its comments including the action taken or proposed; (f) publish the inquiry report with the comments and the action taken. The Act thus gives the NHRC **only the power to recommend** — the 'recommendatory' character that its critics and the Commission itself (the 1998 and 2018 proposals for binding powers; the Supreme Court in the Gujarat 2002 case accepted its reports as the basis of its orders) identify as its weakness; but the Government's practice has been to accept its compensation recommendations in the great majority of cases (the NHRC's annual reports show acceptance rates above ninety per cent, with the Armed Forces and some States as exceptions), and the Commission's standing to move the courts (Sec 18(b) — NHRC v. State of Arunachal Pradesh, 1996; the Gujarat riot cases, 2003-04, where the NHRC secured the transfer of the Best Bakery trial) gives its findings a judicial route. **Sec 19 — the armed forces**: on a complaint of violation by members of the armed forces (the army, navy, air force and the paramilitary forces under the Union), the Commission may only seek a report from the Central Government and, on its receipt, either not proceed or make its recommendations to the Government, which must inform it within three months of the action taken, and the Commission publishes the report — the provision that has kept the AFSPA areas' deaths outside the Commission's inquiry, and the subject of the 2006-19 demands for its deletion (the Jeevan Reddy Committee, 2005, and the Verma Committee, 2013, recommended the Commission's full jurisdiction). **Sec 20** — annual and special reports to Parliament with the Government's memorandum of action taken and reasons for non-acceptance. **Chapter V — the State Human Rights Commissions**: **Sec 21** — constitution (a Chairperson who has been a Chief Justice or a Judge of a High Court (2019); one Member who is or has been a Judge of a High Court or a District Judge with seven years' experience; one Member from persons with knowledge or experience of human rights); functions under Sec 12(a)-(d), (e)-(j) with reference to matters in List II and III (Sec 21(5)) — a complaint on a List I matter goes to the NHRC; two or more States may share a Commission (2006); **Sec 22** — appointment by the Governor on the recommendation of the Chief Minister (chair), the Speaker, the Home Minister, the Leader of the Opposition (and the Chairman and Leader of the Opposition of the Council); **Secs 23-29** — removal, term, staff, procedure (Secs 9-16 and 17-18 apply mutatis mutandis); the reports to the Legislature. All States except a few (Arunachal, Mizoram, Nagaland — the 2019 amendment allows the NHRC to cover UTs) have SHRCs; the A.P. SHRC (1993, revived after bifurcation in 2019 at Kurnool) and the Telangana SHRC (2019, Hyderabad) are the local bodies. **Chapter VI — Human Rights Courts**: **Sec 30** — for the purpose of providing speedy trial of offences arising out of violation of human rights, the State Government may, with the concurrence of the Chief Justice of the High Court, by notification specify for each district a Court of Session to be a Human Rights Court — no separate courts or procedure were created, no 'offences of violation of human rights' are defined, and the notified courts (Andhra Pradesh notified its Sessions Courts in 1994-95; most States have) have tried no distinctive cases — the Act's dead letter; **Sec 31** — a Special Public Prosecutor of seven years' practice. **Chapter VII — finance, accounts and audit** (Secs 32-35 — grants from the Consolidated Fund; the CAG's audit); **Chapter VIII — miscellaneous**: **Sec 36 — matters not subject to jurisdiction**: (1) the Commission shall not inquire into any matter which is pending before a State Commission or any other Commission duly constituted under any law; (2) **the Commission or the State Commission shall not inquire into any matter after the expiry of one year from the date on which the act constituting the violation is alleged to have been committed** — the limitation the Commissions apply with some flexibility and that the 2019 amendment left untouched; **Sec 37** — the Government's power to constitute special investigation teams; **Sec 38** — protection of action taken in good faith; **Sec 39** — members and officers as public servants; **Sec 40-41** — rules and regulations; **Sec 42** — removal of difficulties; **Sec 43** — repeal of the ordinance. **The NHRC's work**: over a lakh complaints a year (custodial deaths — the 1993 direction that every custodial death be reported within twenty-four hours, and the 1997 guidelines on post-mortems and videography; encounter deaths — the 1997 and 2010 guidelines, and the Supreme Court's PUCL v. State of Maharashtra (2014) directions; the Punjab 'mass cremations' case (1995-2006 — the Supreme Court referred the 2,097 cremations to the NHRC under Art 32, which awarded compensation in over 1,500 cases); the Gujarat 2002 report and interventions; the Manipur 2004 and 2023 inquiries; the 2018 Bhima Koregaon; the bonded-labour and silicosis monitoring under the Supreme Court's orders; the mental-hospital visits; the Covid migrant advisories; the 2020-24 advisories on prisons, the elderly, the transgender persons), the Paris Principles' 'A' status through the Global Alliance of NHRIs (first accredited 1999; re-accredited 2006, 2011, 2017; the review deferred in 2023 and 2024 over the appointment process's lack of transparency and pluralism, the police officers in investigation, the Sec 19 exclusion and the absence of women and minority members — the first time India's status has been questioned; A status retained pending review in 2025). **The other institutions**: the National Commissions for Women (1990), Minorities (1992), SCs and STs (Arts 338, 338A — constitutional), Backward Classes (2018 — Art 338B), the Protection of Child Rights (2005), Safai Karamcharis (1993), the Chief Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities (1995/2016), the Central Vigilance Commission, the Lokpal (2013), the National Legal Services Authority (1987 — Art 39A), the Central and State Information Commissions (2005), the Police Complaints Authorities (Prakash Singh, 2006), and the Human Rights Cells in police headquarters — the 'plurality' the Paris Principles contemplate, and the source of the overlap that the 2006 'deemed member' amendment tried to manage."));
c.push(H3("An assessment"));
c.push(P("**The strengths** of the Indian mechanism: a written, justiciable Bill of Rights with a wide Art 21; the Supreme Court's original writ jurisdiction and the PIL; the incorporation of the international instruments by interpretation; the constitutional commissions; a statutory NHRC with quasi-judicial powers and a record of compensation and guidelines; a free press and an active civil society; the ratification of most of the core treaties. **The weaknesses**: the non-ratification of the Torture Convention (signed 1997) and of the optional protocols (no international individual complaint lies against India); the absence of domestic legislation on torture (the 2010 Bill lapsed; the Law Commission's 273rd Report, 2017), on genocide and crimes against humanity, on refugees, on witness protection (the 2018 Supreme Court scheme), on whistle-blowers (the 2014 Act not in force), and on police reform (the Prakash Singh directions largely ignored); the AFSPA and the Sec 19 exclusion; the NHRC's recommendatory powers, its one-year limitation, its dependence on Government investigators, its appointment process, and its perceived reticence (the 2023-24 GANHRI deferral); the SHRCs' vacancies and resources; the pendency of the courts (5 crore cases) that makes the fair-trial and speedy-trial rights illusory; the undertrial population (75 per cent of prisoners); the custodial deaths (about 2,000 a year including judicial custody) and the low conviction of police; the preventive-detention laws (the NSA, the UAPA's bail standard — Watali, 2019; the PMLA's — Vijay Madanlal, 2022); the treatment of dissent and the FCRA; the caste and communal violence; the gaps in women's and children's protection. The international scrutiny — the UPR's 2022 recommendations, the Human Rights Committee's 2024 concluding observations, the CERD's and CEDAW's pending reviews, the Special Rapporteurs' communications — and the domestic scrutiny — the NHRC's reports, the courts, the press, the NGOs — are the two channels through which the Vienna Declaration's 'first responsibility of Governments' is measured. The examiner wants the student to know both the machinery and its critique."));
c.push(CASE("Chairman, Railway Board v. Chandrima Das (2000) 2 SCC 465","Art 21 protects non-citizens; rape by railway employees is a violation of the right to life with dignity; the UDHR's principles applied through Art 51(c); the State's constitutional liability to compensate under Arts 32 and 226 is a public-law remedy distinct from vicarious liability in tort."));
c.push(CASE("Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) 6 SCC 241; Gramophone Co of India v. Birendra Bahadur Pandey (1984) 2 SCC 534","The rules for using international law in Indian courts: customary law is part of domestic law unless inconsistent with statute; conventions not inconsistent with the fundamental rights are read into them; the Court fills the legislative gap."));
c.push(CASE("Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993) 2 SCC 746 and D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (1997) 1 SCC 416","Compensation for custodial death as a constitutional remedy (Art 9(5) ICCPR); the eleven arrest and detention requirements, now in Secs 41A-41D and 50-A CrPC/BNSS 35-38 and 48."));
c.push(CASE("National Human Rights Commission v. State of Arunachal Pradesh (1996) 1 SCC 742","The NHRC's standing under Sec 18(b) to move the Supreme Court; the Chakmas' Art 21 rights; the State's duty to protect."));
c.push(CASE("Paramjit Kaur v. State of Punjab (1996) 7 SCC 20 and (1999) 2 SCC 131","The Punjab mass-cremations matter referred by the Supreme Court to the NHRC under Art 32; the NHRC's jurisdiction in such a reference is sui generis and not limited by the Act's one-year rule or Sec 19; compensation awarded in over 1,500 cases."));
c.push(CASE("Extra Judicial Execution Victim Families Association v. Union of India (2016) 14 SCC 536","The 1,528 alleged encounter deaths in Manipur to be investigated; the AFSPA does not confer immunity for excessive force; the armed forces' 'dos and don'ts'; the NHRC's role and the CBI's inquiry; the Sec 19 limitation criticised."));
c.push(CASE("Puttaswamy v. Union of India (2017) 10 SCC 1","Privacy as a fundamental right under Arts 14, 19 and 21; ADM Jabalpur overruled; Art 17 ICCPR and Art 12 UDHR as interpretive sources; the proportionality test for restrictions — the modern statement of the constitutional human-rights method."));
c.push(DIG("The NHRC's HRCNet portal receives and tracks complaints online (with the SHRCs on the same platform); its 2023 advisories cover digital rights and cybercrime victims; the Supreme Court's e-Committee and live-streaming apply the open-justice principle; the NALSA's Tele-Law scheme delivers Art 39A digitally; and the GANHRI accreditation reports are public — the 2023-24 deferral documents are the newest source on the NHRC's standing."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Definition of human rights under the PHR Act', 'Composition of the NHRC (after 2019)', 'Functions of the NHRC (Sec 12)', 'Powers of the NHRC (Sec 13)', 'Sec 18 — steps after inquiry', 'Sec 19 — armed forces', 'State Human Rights Commission', 'Human Rights Courts (Sec 30)', 'Sec 36 — one-year limitation', 'Paris Principles', 'Art 51(c)', 'Art 253', 'Chandrima Das case', 'Public interest litigation'. Part B: 'Explain the constitutional mechanism for the implementation of international human-rights law in India, with reference to Art 51(c), Art 253 and the leading cases'; or 'Describe the composition, functions and powers of the National Human Rights Commission under the Protection of Human Rights Act 1993 and evaluate its working'; or 'Discuss the State Human Rights Commissions and the Human Rights Courts'; or 'Is the NHRC a toothless tiger? Examine with reference to Secs 18, 19 and 36 and the 2019 amendment'. Part C: a foreign tourist is assaulted by policemen — Art 21 applies to non-citizens (Chandrima Das, Chakma), compensation under Art 226, NHRC complaint within a year; a custodial death occurs in a paramilitary camp — Sec 19, the NHRC may only seek a report; the courts and the CBI remain (EEVFAM); a complaint is filed with the NHRC two years after the violation — Sec 36(2) bars it, unless the Supreme Court refers it (Paramjit Kaur) or a continuing violation is shown; a State refuses to act on the NHRC's compensation recommendation — Sec 18(e)-(f) publication, Sec 18(b) writ, the Government's reasons to Parliament under Sec 20; a court is asked to apply the Torture Convention which India has only signed — not binding, but the customary prohibition and Art 21 (Puttaswamy; D.K. Basu) give the same result."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- DRILL BANK ----------
c.push(H1("Part C Drill Bank — 10 Problems with IRAC Answers"));
c.push(P("Write each answer as Issue → Rule (instrument, article and case) → Application → Conclusion. Ten to twelve lines each in the exam."));
const drills=[
["1. The order to fire","During a coup, a colonel orders his battalion to fire on a crowd of unarmed protesters; a captain who refuses is shot; a lieutenant obeys and forty die. The colonel later says he acted under the junta's decree; the lieutenant says he obeyed orders.","**Issue:** Individual responsibility and the defences of national law and superior orders. **Rule:** Nuremberg Principles II-IV (internal law and superior orders no defence); Rome Statute Arts 7 (crimes against humanity — a widespread or systematic attack on a civilian population), 25, 27, 33 (orders to commit crimes against humanity are manifestly unlawful); Art 6 ICCPR; Velásquez Rodríguez on the State's duty. **Application:** Firing on unarmed protesters as part of a coup is murder as a crime against humanity if widespread or systematic; the decree is no defence; the lieutenant's order was manifestly unlawful. **Conclusion:** Both are criminally responsible internationally (the ICC if the State is a party or the Council refers; universal jurisdiction otherwise); the State is responsible under the Covenant; the captain's killing is an additional crime."],
["2. The Rohingya family","A Rohingya family registered with UNHCR in Delhi is detained under the Foreigners Act and scheduled for deportation to Myanmar, where the ICJ has ordered provisional measures under the Genocide Convention.","**Issue:** Non-refoulement in India. **Rule:** India is not a party to the 1951 Convention; Art 33 is customary law; Art 3 CAT (signed) and Arts 6-7 ICCPR (ratified) prohibit return to torture or death (Chahal; Soering; GC 31); Art 21 applies to non-citizens (Chakma; Ktaer Abbas — non-refoulement within Art 21); Mohammad Salimullah (2021) — deportation permitted by procedure, the right not to be deported concomitant to Art 19(1)(e); The Gambia v. Myanmar's provisional measures show the risk. **Application:** The deportation would return them to a State under ICJ orders for genocide-risk; Salimullah leaves the Art 21 question open; the risk is real and individualised. **Conclusion:** A writ under Art 226/32 should seek a stay on Art 21 grounds with the ICJ's findings; at minimum the procedure (verification by Myanmar, no deportation of asylum-seekers under UNHCR cards) must be followed; the case is arguable both ways after Salimullah."],
["3. The women's-only bar rule","A State law bars women under twenty-five from working in premises serving liquor 'for their safety'. A hotel workers' union challenges it.","**Issue:** Protective discrimination vs stereotype. **Rule:** Art 15(1) and (3), Art 19(1)(g); CEDAW Arts 2, 5(a), 11 and GR 25 (special measures must aim at equality, not at protection based on stereotypes); Anuj Garg v. Hotel Association (2008) — the same law struck down as perpetuating stereotypes; Babita Puniya (2020). **Application:** The law restricts women's employment on paternalistic grounds; the State must secure safety by policing, not exclusion; it does not meet Art 4 CEDAW's test of a temporary special measure. **Conclusion:** Unconstitutional; the State's Art 15(3) power cannot be used to disadvantage women."],
["4. The mine and the gram sabha","A company obtains a lease for bauxite mining in a Scheduled Area of Andhra Pradesh; the gram sabhas were 'informed' but not consulted; the tribals worship a hill in the lease area.","**Issue:** Consent and consultation of indigenous communities. **Rule:** Fifth Schedule and the A.P. Land Transfer Regulation (Samatha — leases to non-tribals void, 'person' includes Government); PESA Sec 4(i) (consultation before acquisition); Forest Rights Act Secs 3(1)(e), 4(d), 5 and 6 (the gram sabha's authority; habitat rights; religious and cultural rights); Niyamgiri (2013) — the gram sabha decides whether the project infringes those rights; ILO 169 Arts 6, 15 and UNDRIP Arts 19, 32 (FPIC) as persuasive; the 2013 Land Acquisition Act Sec 41. **Application:** 'Information' is not consultation; the hill's religious significance triggers the FRA process; the lease to a company is barred by the Regulation if the company is a non-tribal. **Conclusion:** The lease is void under Samatha; even otherwise, the Niyamgiri procedure must be followed and the gram sabhas' decision respected."],
["5. The confession by video","A country's army captures an enemy soldier, broadcasts his interrogation on television, and after the ceasefire keeps him for eighteen months pending 'a comprehensive settlement'.","**Issue:** POW protections. **Rule:** GC III Art 13 (no exposure to public curiosity), Art 17 (only name, rank, number; no coercion), Art 118 (release and repatriation without delay after the cessation of active hostilities); the Abhinandan (2019) and 1971 precedents; grave breaches under Art 130 for wilful deprivation of rights; the ICRC's Art 126 access. **Application:** The broadcast breaches Arts 13 and 17; the detention after the ceasefire for political reasons breaches Art 118 (the 1971 argument that a peace settlement was needed was widely criticised). **Conclusion:** The detaining State is in breach; the captured soldier's State may invoke common Art 1, the Protecting Power, the ICRC, and the Security Council; the individuals responsible for the broadcast and prolonged detention may be prosecuted for grave breaches."],
["6. The critic in prison","A blogger is jailed for two years under a 'false news' law for criticising the President's economic policy; he applies to the Human Rights Committee.","**Issue:** Admissibility and merits under the ICCPR. **Rule:** Art 19(2)-(3) and GC 34 — restrictions must be provided by law, necessary and proportionate to a listed aim; criticism of public figures is protected; 'false news' laws are incompatible; Art 9; the First Optional Protocol's admissibility (Art 1 — the State must be a party; Art 5(2)(b) — exhaustion; not under another procedure). **Application:** If his State is a Protocol party and he has exhausted remedies, the communication is admissible; a two-year sentence for policy criticism fails necessity and proportionality (GC 34 para 47; the Committee's Belarus and Cameroon views). **Conclusion:** A finding of violation of Arts 19 and 9, with release and compensation recommended; if the State is not a Protocol party (as India is not), the remedy is the Special Rapporteur on freedom of expression's communication and the Working Group on Arbitrary Detention."],
["7. The extradition to the death penalty","India seeks extradition from a European State of an accused in a terrorism case punishable with death; the requested State's court refuses unless India gives assurances.","**Issue:** Non-refoulement and the death penalty in extradition. **Rule:** ECHR Arts 2 and 3 with Protocols 6 and 13 (Soering; Öcalan; Al-Saadoon — no extradition to face the death penalty); Chahal (Art 3 absolute); the EU Charter Art 19(2); the requested State's extradition law; India's practice of giving assurances (the Abu Salem assurance to Portugal, 2002 — honoured by the Supreme Court in Abu Salem v. State of Maharashtra, 2022, capping the sentence at twenty-five years). **Application:** Without an assurance that the death penalty will not be imposed or executed, and on prison conditions (the Nirav Modi and Sanjay Bhandari cases), extradition will be refused. **Conclusion:** India must give a binding assurance; the Indian courts will hold the Government to it (Abu Salem)."],
["8. The NHRC's recommendation ignored","The NHRC finds a custodial death in a State police station, recommends ₹10 lakh compensation and prosecution of three constables; the State does nothing for a year.","**Issue:** Enforcement of the NHRC's findings. **Rule:** PHR Act Sec 18(a)(i)-(ii), (b), (e), (f); Sec 20; Sec 13's inquiry powers; Nilabati Behera and D.K. Basu (compensation under Art 32/226); NHRC v. Arunachal Pradesh (standing); the 1993 custodial-death reporting direction; Art 9(5) ICCPR. **Application:** The Commission may publish the report with the State's non-compliance, move the High Court under Sec 18(b) for a writ directing payment and prosecution, and report to Parliament; the victim's family may itself petition under Art 226 relying on the NHRC's findings. **Conclusion:** The recommendation is not self-executing, but the writ route (Sec 18(b)) and the courts' public-law compensation jurisprudence make it enforceable in practice."],
["9. The State that refuses the ICRC","In an internal insurgency, a State detains hundreds of suspected insurgents in army camps, denies the ICRC access and tries some before military courts without counsel.","**Issue:** Applicable law in a non-international armed conflict. **Rule:** Common Art 3 (humane treatment; no sentences without a regularly constituted court with judicial guarantees; the ICRC may offer services); AP II Arts 5-6 if applicable; the Tadić threshold; Art 9, 10 and 14 ICCPR and GC 29 and 35 (derogation limits); the Geneva Conventions Act's Sec 3 for grave breaches (in IAC); the NHRC's Sec 19 limits; the Human Rights Committee's 2024 observations on India's AFSPA. **Application:** If the violence meets the intensity and organisation thresholds, common Art 3 binds; the trials violate Art 3(1)(d) and Art 14 ICCPR; the State may refuse the ICRC but bears the consequences; human-rights law applies concurrently. **Conclusion:** Violations of common Art 3 and the Covenant; the detainees are entitled to habeas corpus and counsel; the international remedies are the Council's special procedures, the treaty bodies and, if applicable, the ICC."],
["10. The company and the river","A multinational's factory discharges toxic waste into a river in a State party to the ICESCR; villagers lose their water and health; the State does nothing.","**Issue:** The State's duty to protect economic and social rights against private actors; business responsibility. **Rule:** ICESCR Arts 11-12 and GC 14-15 (the duty to protect against third parties); the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights (2011 — the State duty to protect, the corporate responsibility to respect, access to remedy); SERAC v. Nigeria; the Indian Art 21 environmental cases (M.C. Mehta; Vellore Citizens — the polluter-pays and precautionary principles; Subhash Kumar — the right to pollution-free water) and the National Green Tribunal Act 2010 Sec 15. **Application:** The State's inaction breaches its duty to protect; the company breaches its responsibility to respect and is liable under national environmental law; the villagers have the NGT and Art 226 remedies and, internationally, the CESCR's review and the OP if the State is a party. **Conclusion:** State responsibility under the Covenant; corporate liability domestically; the Guiding Principles as the standard for the company's due diligence."]
];
